==Trivia==
*The Japanese names reference two different songs about hometowns:
**The sub-chapter's Japanese name, Neko Oishi Furusato (猫追いしふるさと, "Cat-Chasing Hometown"), is a reference to the children's song "[[wikipedia:Furusato|Furusato]]". Neko Oishi is taken from the lyric "''usagi oishi ka no yama''" ("I chased after rabbits on that mountain"), but replaces ''usagi'' (rabbit) with ''neko'' (cat). Furusato is both the name of the song and a word found in several of the lyrics; it means "old country home".
**[[Outta This Village]]'s Japanese name, Ora Konna Kokyō Deruda (おらこんな故郷出るだ, "I'm Leaving My Hometown"), is a reference to the [[wikipedia:Yoshi Ikuzō|Yoshi Ikuzō]] song "[[wikipedia:Ora Tōkyō sa Iguda|Ora Tōkyō sa Iguda]]" (俺ら東京さ行ぐだ, "I'm Going to Tokyo"), in which the singer declares that he will leave his small hometown in the countryside to move to Tokyo.
*Other than hometowns, Cat-Chasing Village also has a theme of ancient history, befitting the increased focus on [[:Category:Relic Enemies|Relics]]:
**[[Pithouse Academy]] mentions [[wikipedia:Pithouse|pithouses]], a prehistoric primitive shelter built mostly or entirely below ground and roofed over.
**[[Clay Pot Soul Food]]'s Japanese name, Kaengata Doki de Ofukuro no Aji (火焔型土器でおふくろの味, Mom's-cooking-taste Flame-style Earthenware"), mentions ''[https://youtu.be/mKxCi8YiT2E kaengata doki]'' (火焔型土器, "flame-style earthenware"), a type of pottery from Japan's prehistoric [[wikipedia:Jōmon period|Jōmon period]].
**[[Haniwanwan]] is based on ''[[wikipedia:Haniwa|haniwa]]'', funerary clay figurines from the [[wikipedia:Kofun period|Kofun period]] of Japan.

